<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Eshnunna</h1><p class="st-meta">2028 BCE – 1762 BCE (approx.) · capital Eshnunna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Amorite-era kingdom in the Diyala valley whose law code is one of the oldest in the world and whose kings rivalled Hammurabi of Babylon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eshnunna, modern Tell Asmar, had been a city since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> and a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>. As Ur collapsed around 2028 BCE its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilshu-iliya">Ilshu-iliya</a> declared independence, and the city became the centre of a kingdom controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala</a> valley and the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its god was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tishpak">Tishpak</a>, and its rulers styled themselves his servants before claiming full kingship. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipiq-adad-ii">Ipiq-Adad II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadusha">Dadusha</a> in the 19th and early 18th centuries BCE it expanded to the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-of-eshnunna">Laws of Eshnunna</a>, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-harmal">Tell Harmal</a> (ancient Shaduppum), set prices, wages and penalties in Akkadian and predate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi's</a> code. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a> excavations in the 1930s also found the Tell Asmar hoard of praying statues.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Eshnunna fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> for supremacy. In 1762 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> defeated it and it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a>; a later year name of his records the destruction of Eshnunna by flood waters.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eshnunna.jpg" alt="Eshnunna" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_head_Tell_Asmas_Louvre_AO12434.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2028 BCE – 1762 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eshnunna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ipiq-Adad II, Naram-Sin of Eshnunna, Dadusha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Eshnunna, Tell Asmar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1895&amp;focus=eshnunna" aria-label="Eshnunna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5822 954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eshnunna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6142 1210L6193 1305M6142 1210L6143 1252"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6193 1305h0M6143 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6142 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6142 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1895&amp;focus=eshnunna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
